# Rust build output
/target/
**/*.rs.bk

# Cargo.lock is COMMITTED, deliberately. Do not add it here.
#
# The usual "libraries omit it, binaries commit it" rule already points that way, since
# this workspace ships overtone-cli. But the stronger reason is Part I 5: same seed, same
# trajectory. A run someone reproduces from a permalink has to resolve the same dependency
# versions we did, and an unpinned lockfile quietly breaks that.

# WASM build output (Phase 4). wasm-pack writes pkg/ next to the crate it builds.
/crates/overtone-wasm/pkg/
/web/pkg/
/web/node_modules/

# Oracle cases are generated from the Rust engine on demand and never edited by hand:
#   cargo run -q -p overtone-sim --example emit_oracle_cases > lab/test/cases.txt
# Committing them would let the fixture drift out of step with the engine it came from,
# which is the one failure mode a differential test must not have.
/lab/test/cases.txt

# Julia coverage and profiling artefacts.
#
# lab/Manifest.toml is COMMITTED, deliberately. CI runs Pkg.instantiate() against it, so
# pinning it is what makes "gradients match Yao.jl to 1e-12" a claim about a specific Yao
# version rather than about whatever resolved that morning.
*.jl.cov
*.jl.*.cov
*.jl.mem
/lab/docs/build/
/lab/docs/site/

# Generated figures are committed (Part I 5 lists figures/ as a deliverable used by the
# README), so nothing is ignored here. Left as a note so nobody adds figures/ later.

# Editor and OS noise
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/

# Local tool indexes are NOT listed here. .codegraph/, graphify-out/ and .serena/ are
# per-machine derived state, and .gitignore is a tracked file, so putting them here would
# push one developer's tooling choices into everyone else's diff. They live in
# .git/info/exclude instead, which is local and untracked.
